explanations.E0043:
# E0043: expression without a value

An expression whose value is used must have one. A call of a function that returns nothing has none, and neither has an `if` without an `else`. An expression that never finishes, such as an `if` whose branches all `return`, has none either. Nor has a bare `break` in a `loop` whose other `break`s give it a value.

For a string literal, the error underlines the first part of the literal that never finishes.

## Why

A `let`, an argument, an operand or a condition needs a value to work with. Nothing is left to bind when the expression returns nothing, or never returns.

## Broken

```sisu
fn greet() {
    print(1)
}

fn main() {
    let x = greet()
    print(x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn one() -> i64 {
    1
}

fn main() {
    let x = one()
    print(x)
}
```

## Related

- [W0001](W0001.md): a statement that follows one that never finishes.

explanations.E0053:
# E0053: call with the wrong number of arguments

A call passes one argument for each parameter of the function or method, and a variant's constructor one for each field of its payload: `Expr.Num(1)`. The array methods `len()` and `sum()` take none and `contains(x)` takes one; a conversion `i64(...)` or `u8(...)` takes one, and so do `panic(...)` and `todo(...)`; `read_line()` takes none.

The error names a method as the receiver's type and the method: `i64.min`, `f64.sqrt`, `string.len`, `[i64].contains`, `Box<i64>.get`. The type is the one that the call has. It is not the type that the `extend` block writes. A call on a `[U]` names `[U].contains`, not `[T].contains`. A call on a type parameter through a bound names the interface of the bound: `Show.show`. A function has no receiver, and the error names it alone: `add`.

## Why

Sisu has no default values for parameters and no functions that take a varying number of arguments. Each parameter needs its value, and a value with no parameter has nowhere to go.

## Broken

```sisu
fn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
    a + b
}

fn main() {
    print(add(1))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
    a + b
}

fn main() {
    print(add(1, 2))
}
```

## Related

- [E0064](E0064.md): a constructor with more arguments than the class or variant has fields.

explanations.E0059:
# E0059: `print`, `eprint` or `dbg` of something it cannot print

`print` and `eprint` take exactly one value, and write it on its own line: `print` to standard output, `eprint` to standard error. `dbg` takes exactly one value too: it writes the value to standard error with its position and source text, and returns it. The message names the function that was called, and it says which rule the call breaks.

## Why

A call that returns nothing has no value to print: the message says that its argument has no value. A call with no argument, or with several, is not a call with one value: the message says that the function takes one value. The rules for `dbg` are the rules for `print`: `dbg(greet())` of a call that returns nothing, `dbg()` and `dbg(1, 2)` are all this error.

## Broken

```sisu
fn greet() {
    print(1)
}

fn main() {
    print(greet())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn greet() -> string {
    "hello"
}

fn main() {
    print(greet())
}
```

## Related

- [W0015](W0015.md): `print` or `eprint` of an optional.

explanations.E0063:
# E0063: field or method that does not exist

A field read or a method call must name a member of the receiver's type: a field or method of its class, the array methods `len()`, `sum()` and `contains(x)`, the `string` methods `len()`, `chars()`, `bytes()` and `words()`, or the methods of `f64` and `i64`, such as `x.sqrt()` and `n.max(m)`. A built-in type's name takes a constant: `f64.pi`, `f64.tau`, `f64.e`, `f64.infinity`, `f64.nan`, `f64.max`, `f64.min_positive` and `f64.epsilon`. No other type has any, so `i64.max` is this error too. A method of `f64` is no method of `i64`: `n.sqrt()` with an `i64` `n` is this error too. A constructor's labels must name fields of its class, or of the labeled payload of its variant. The methods of an enum count as methods of its type, but its value has no fields: that is [E0121](E0121.md).

When a member of the receiver's type has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a member that was never written. For `let x = 2.5` and `x.sqrtt()`, the unsafe fix is `sqrt`; for `f64.PI`, it is `pi`.

The same error is reported for a method call on a value of a type parameter: the type parameter has only the methods of its bounds. When exactly one interface of the program declares a method of that name, and it takes no type parameters, the help adds the bound: for `x.area()` on a value of type `T`, `add a bound: T: Shape`. If `T` has bounds already, the help lists them with the new one.

## Why

Sisu does not resolve a misspelled member name on its own. A misspelled name would otherwise read some other value or run some other code.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

explanations.E0064:
# E0064: constructor with more arguments than fields

A constructor names every field of its class once, in declaration order: `Point(x: 1)` for a class with the one field `x`. A variant's constructor does the same for its payload: `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)` for a variant with two labeled fields.

## Why

Each argument of a constructor sets one field. An argument after the last field has no field to set.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(p.x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(p.x + p.y)
}
```

## Related

- [E0056](E0056.md): a constructor without every field.

explanations.E0065:
# E0065: constructor argument without a label

Each argument of a constructor is labeled with the field it sets: `Point(x: 1)`. A variant whose payload is labeled is built the same way: `Shape.Rect(w: 2, h: 3)`. A variant with an unlabeled payload, such as `Num(i64)`, takes its arguments without labels; a label there is [E0055](E0055.md).

## Why

The label says which field gets which value, so a constructor reads the same however many fields its class or variant has, and swapping two values of one type cannot go unnoticed.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

explanations.E0124:
# E0124: `match` on a type it cannot take

`match` takes an enum, an optional, an integer, a `bool`, a `char` or a `string`. A class, an array or an `f64` is not one of them.

## Why

A pattern names a variant or a value that the program can compare exactly. A class has no variants, and an `f64` does not compare exactly. Read the class's fields.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    let n = match p {
        _ => 0
    }
    print(n)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    let n = match p.x {
        1 => p.y
        _ => 0
    }
    print(n)
}
```

explanations.E0130:
# E0130: `match` that does not cover every value

A `match` needs an arm for every value its scrutinee can hold. When a value reaches no arm, the error names one such value as a pattern, at the `match` keyword: "`match` does not cover `Mul(_, _)`". When an arm has the missing value's variant at the top, a label on that arm says which part of the variant it covers: "`Add(Num(_), _)` covers only `Num(_), _`". When a guarded arm may not match the value, a label on the `match` keyword says so: a guarded arm covers nothing, since its guard can be false.

Integers and `char`s are finite, so literals and ranges that together hold every value cover the type: `0..=127` and `128..=255` cover a `u8`. A missing integer or `char` is named as the first gap from the bottom, "`match` does not cover `10..=255`", with the help "add `_ =>`"; so is a `string`, which only `_` or a name covers.

An optional has two values to cover: `None`, and `Some` of each value inside. A missing value of an optional is named as a pattern at the optional's position: "`match` does not cover `None`", "`match` does not cover `Some(None)`" in an optional of an optional, or the missing variant of the enum inside, "`match` does not cover `Square(_)`".

## Why

A `match` is an expression, so it must produce a value, or leave, for every input. Checking coverage when the program compiles turns a forgotten variant into an error at the `match`, instead of a crash when that value arrives. Rust and Swift reject such a `match` too. Adding a variant to an enum then points at every `match` that must handle it.

The unsafe fix adds `_ => todo` as the last arm. It is unsafe because a catch-all also takes every variant added later, which then goes unnoticed.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
        Mul(a, b) => eval(a) * eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

## Related

- [W0006](W0006.md): an arm that no value reaches.

explanations.W0003:
# W0003: `todo` left in the program

A `todo` marks code that is not written yet. It panics when it runs, with the message `not yet implemented`, or `not yet implemented: <message>` for `todo("message")`. The warning points at each `todo`, and the program still builds.

## Why

A `todo` lets a function type-check before its body exists: it is an expression of type `never`, so it stands in for any value. A program that still holds one builds, but it should not ship that way, so the compiler says where each one is. Rust's `todo!()` does not warn, and Clippy's `todo` lint is off by default; Sisu warns because the next run that reaches the `todo` will fail.

## Broken

```sisu
fn area(width: i64, height: i64) -> i64 {
    todo("multiply")
}

fn main() {
    print(area(2, 3))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn area(width: i64, height: i64) -> i64 {
    width * height
}

fn main() {
    print(area(2, 3))
}
```
